  "market_commentary": {
    "overview": "",
    "bulkers": "The charter market experienced a significant downturn this week as the Baltic Dry Index (BDI) \ndeclined for 4th consecutive week closing today at 2,511 points, reflecting a slump of almost 14 % \ncompared to last Friday's closing value of 2,917 points: \n \nBaltic Dry Index   \n2511 -34 \nBaltic Capesize Index  \n3249 -73 \nBaltic Panamax Index  \n2314 -42 \nBaltic Supramax Index  \n1865 +9 \nBaltic Handysize Index  \n1011 +4 \n \nAlthough charter rates hit an over-one-month low, the bulker S+P market is particularly active with a \ntotal of 16 transactions recorded, comprising 2 panamax, 4 ultramaxes, 3 supramaxes, 1 handymax and \n6 handysize bulk carriers.  \n \nFor a detailed review and additional information regarding these week's transactions, kindly refer to our \n\"representative sales\" section.",
    "demolition": "The Subcontinent markets remain stable this week, with minimal availability of new \ntonnage. Despite a decline in local steel prices, demo rates are largely unchanged. Indian market \nsentiment is positive, with a notable demand for specialized vessels, including stainless steel tankers and \nRoPax vessels. Cash buyers are willing to offer a significant premium for these types of ships. For an \noverview of demolition market rates, please check our comprehensive demometer below:"
  },
